 ],
 "literal": "Plucked greens to wither, brought firewood to dry / with the peacock-like graceful dark-skinned woman / it is hungry indeed, the great-natured town / The house-dwelling sparrow, the black-throated male / with the split fibers of the bards' lute strings, and strong animal's / voice-like peacock feathers, built nest / eating the rice of the big wet-field paddy, with its / grey-backed female dwells / it will be prosperous, if the king encounters distress.",
 "translation": "While plucked greens wither and brought firewood dries, along with the dark-skinned woman of peacock-like grace, the town of the noble warrior is hungry indeed. But the black-throated male sparrow, dwelling in the house, having built its nest with the shredded fibers of the bards' lute strings and the soft tips of peacock feathers, feeds on the rice of the great wet fields and dwells there with its grey-backed female. Such prosperity will come to this town, if the king encounters distress in war.",
 "modern": "பறித்த கீரைகள் வாடவும், கொண்டுவந்த விறகுகள் காயவும், மயிலைப் போன்ற மென்மையையும் கரிய நிறத்தையும் உடைய தன் மனைவியோடு அந்தப் பெருந்தகை வாழும் ஊர் பசியோடு இருக்கிறது. ஆனால், வீட்டில் வாழும் கரிய தொண்டையையுடைய ஆண் சிட்டுக்குருவி, பாணர்களுடைய யாழ் நரம்பின் நார் போன்ற இழைகளையும், மயில்தோகையின் மெல்லிய இறகுகளையும் கொண்டு கட்டிய கூட்டில், பெரிய வயல் நெல்லின் அரிசியை உண்டு, தன் சாம்பல் நிறப் பெண் குருவியோடு வாழ்கிறது. வேந்தனுக்குப் போர் போன்ற துன்பம் வரும்போது, இவ்வூர் பெரும் செல்வ வளத்தைப் பெறும்.",
 "explanation": "The poet Peruṅkuṉṟūr Kiḻār sings in the theme of vallāṇ mullai (the strength and hospitality of a warrior's home) under the vākai tiṇai (victory/nobility). The poem describes the humble yet proud state of a generous warrior's village. Currently, the warrior and his graceful wife live in poverty, with only withered greens and dry firewood, and the town itself seems hungry. Yet, even in this poverty, the house sparrows thrive, building nests from the discarded lute strings of visiting bards and peacock feathers, and feeding on fine rice. This contrast implies that the warrior's household is always open to bards and guests, sharing whatever they have. The poet concludes that when the king faces a crisis or war, this brave warrior will go to battle, win great spoils, and restore immense prosperity to his town.",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
 "generated": "2026-11-26"
}